# Blockchain Basics: Trust Without a Middleman

> A blockchain lets strangers who do not trust each other share one
> tamper-evident ledger — no bank in the middle. It is less an
> invention than a clever assembly of known parts: hash functions,
> digital signatures and a consensus rule that makes rewriting
> history cost more than it could ever pay.

## 1. The Problem Blockchains Solve

Alice pays Bob through a bank because the bank keeps the ledger
everyone believes. Remove the bank and any peer could claim "Alice
paid me a million" — who arbitrates? A blockchain makes the ledger
itself the arbiter: replicated across thousands of independent nodes,
each holding an identical copy, with rules for adding entries that no
minority can bend.

| Centralised ledger | Blockchain |
| --- | --- |
| one trusted keeper | many untrusting keepers |
| keeper can censor/reverse | entries effectively irreversible |
| hours to settle | minutes (or seconds) |
| single point of failure | survives node loss |

## 2. Hash Functions: The Tamper-Seal

[definition]
A **cryptographic hash** (SHA-256) converts any input into a fixed
256-bit fingerprint with three properties: the same input always
yields the same hash; a tiny input change scrambles the hash
completely (avalanche effect); and the original cannot be recovered
from the hash (one-wayness).
[/definition]

Change one comma in a document and its hash changes beyond
recognition — which is why hashes are the perfect seal. If a block's
content changes, its hash changes, and every later block that embeds
that hash breaks visibly.

## 3. Blocks, Chains and the Chain of Seals

A block bundles transactions plus:

- the **hash of the previous block** — welding blocks into a chain;
- a timestamp and a **nonce** (a number miners fiddle with);
- a **Merkle root** — a single hash summarising all transactions in
  the block, so membership of any transaction can be proved with a
  short logarithmic path.

@diagram network id="chain-links" layout="hierarchical" aria="Three blocks linked in a chain; each block stores the hash of the previous block"
node b1 [label="Block 1"]
node b2 [label="Block 2 - stores hash of Block 1"]
node b3 [label="Block 3 - stores hash of Block 2"]

edge b1 -> b2
edge b2 -> b3
@/diagram

[important]
To tamper with a transaction in block 100, an attacker must re-mine
block 100 **and every block after it** faster than the honest network
adds new blocks. "Immutability" is not magic — it is economics: the
work needed to rewrite history exceeds any gain from rewriting it,
provided the majority of computing power stays honest.
[/important]

## 4. Public Keys, Signatures and Addresses

Each user holds a **private key** (a secret number) and a **public
key** derived from it. A transaction is signed with the private key;
anyone can verify the signature with the public key without learning
the secret. Addresses are hashes of public keys. "Not your keys, not
your coins" is shorthand for: whoever holds the private key controls
the funds — lose it and no bank can restore access, since there is no
bank.

## 5. Consensus: Agreeing Without a Boss

The network must agree on which new block to append, even with
malicious and slow nodes. Two dominant mechanisms:

| | Proof of Work (PoW) | Proof of Stake (PoS) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Rule | solve a hash puzzle; hardest chain wins | stake coins as collateral; validators chosen by stake |
| Cost | electricity + specialised hardware | capital at risk (slashing for cheating) |
| Used by | Bitcoin | Ethereum (since 2022) |

A **51% attack** — controlling majority hash power or stake — can
censor or reorganise recent blocks, which is why small networks are
easier to attack than Bitcoin. Longest-chain (heaviest-work) rule and
finality gadgets resolve honest forks; brief splits are normal, and
merchants simply wait a few confirmations before trusting a payment.

## 6. Smart Contracts: Programs That Hold Money

[definition]
A **smart contract** is a program stored on the blockchain that
executes exactly as written when its conditions are met — a vending
machine for agreements: put in the input, the code dispenses the
output, no intermediary can interfere.
[/definition]

Simple example: an escrow that releases payment to a seller when an
oracle confirms delivery. Complex reality: DAO hacks and DeFi
exploits (billions lost to reentrancy and logic bugs) prove that
"code is law" cuts both ways — deployed contracts are hard to patch.
Hence the discipline of audits, formal verification and upgradable
proxy patterns.

## 7. Tokens, Coins and the Wider Ecosystem

- **Coins** (BTC, ETH) secure their own chain; **tokens** ride an
  existing one (ERC-20 on Ethereum).
- **Stablecoins** peg value to fiat, trading trustlessness for price
  stability.
- **NFTs** tokenise unique ownership (art, tickets, deeds).
- **Public vs private (permissioned) blockchains**: public = anyone
  can read/write (Bitcoin); private/consortium = known participants
  only (Hyperledger) — common in supply-chain and banking pilots
  where regulation, not censorship-resistance, is the driver.

## 8. Honest Limitations

Throughput remains the Achilles heel — Bitcoin processes ~7
transactions/second versus tens of thousands for card networks —
driving a scaling debate between **layer 1** redesigns and **layer 2**
systems (payment channels, rollups) that batch work off-chain and
settle on-chain. Energy use under PoW is large; PoS cuts it by over
99%. Finality is probabilistic; regulation is unsettled; and a
private key lost is value gone forever. The engineering maturity of
this ecosystem — measured in losses to bugs — is still young.

## 9. Where Blockchains Genuinely Fit

Use a blockchain when multiple parties who do not fully trust one
another need a shared, append-only, verifiable record **and** cannot
anoint a neutral intermediary: cross-border settlement, provenance
tracking (diamonds, drugs, food), land registries, credentials and
censorship-resistant value storage. When a trusted central party
exists and is acceptable, an ordinary database is faster, cheaper and
easier to fix — the honest first question of any blockchain project.
